CONCERNING

IMPLEMENTATION OF PROTECTION

OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

BY THE GRACE OF GOD ALMIGHTY

REGENT OF PENUKAL ABAB LEMATANG ILIR,

Considering Recalling
a. that all forms of violence or treatment that degrades the degree and dignity of women and children constitute a violation of human rights, therefore it is necessary to protect their honor and dignity and guarantee their right to life in accordance with their nature and destiny without discrimination;
b. that violence and treatment that degrades the degree and dignity of women and children must be subject to protection efforts;
c. that based on the considerations as referred to in letters a and b, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Implementation of Protection of Women and Children.
1. Article 18 Paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 4 of 1979 concerning Child Welfare (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1979 Number 32, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3143);
3. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 109, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia _.

Number 4235); as last amended by Law of the Republic of Indonesia Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 297 Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235);

4. Law Number 7 of 2013 concerning the Establishment of Penukal Abab Lematang Ilir Regency in South Sumatra Province (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2013 Number 22, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5400);
5. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587), as has been amended several times most recently by Law Number 9 of 2015 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2015 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5679).
6. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Women's Protection;
7. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;

With Mutual Approval

CHAPTER II

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN

Part One

Rights of Women Article 4 Every Woman has the right:
a. to live, sustain life and her livelihood; b. to form a family and continue offspring through lawful marriage;
c. to develop herself through the fulfillment of her basic needs; d. to obtain education and benefit from science and technology, arts and culture, in order to improve the quality of her life and for the welfare of humankind;
e. to advance herself in fighting for her rights collectively to build society, nation and state;
f. to recognition, guarantee, protection and fair legal certainty as well as equal treatment before the law;
g. to work and receive fair and proper remuneration and treatment in employment relations;
h. to obtain equal opportunities in government;
i. to freedom of association, assembly and expression of opinion;

j. to protection of her person, family, honor, dignity and property under her control, and the right to a sense of security and protection from threats of fear to do or not do something which is a basic right;
k. to be free from torture or treatment that degrades human dignity and the right to obtain political asylum from another country;
l. to live in physical and spiritual prosperity, to reside and obtain a good and healthy living environment, and the right to

obtain health services;

m. to social security that enables her full development as a dignified human being;
n. to equality of position in law and government;
o. to work and a livelihood worthy of humanity;
p. in efforts to defend the state;
q. to communicate and obtain information to develop her personal and social environment, and the right to seek, obtain, possess, store, process, and convey information using all types of available channels;
r. to embrace her respective religion and to worship according to her religion and belief; and s. to obtain education.

Paragraph 1

Rights and Obligations of Children Article 5 Every Child has the right:
a. to birth registration;
b. to obtain health;
c. to obtain education; and d. to social welfare.

Article 6 Every Child has the obligation to:
a. respect parents, guardians and teachers;
b. love family, community and care for friends; c. love the homeland, nation and state;
d. perform worship in accordance with his/her religion and belief;
e. study and develop himself/herself in accordance with his/her abilities and talents, interests; and f. have noble character.

Paragraph 2 Birth Registration

Paragraph 4

Education Article 10 (1) The implementation of Fulfillment of Children's Rights in the field of education as referred to in Article 5 letter c, the Regency Government guarantees the implementation of a 9 (nine) year compulsory education program.

2. Every organizer of an education unit is obliged to provide opportunities for Children to obtain educational services without discrimination.

Article 11 Every organizer of an education unit is prohibited from expelling Children from educational institutions unless there is a guarantee for the continuity of the Child's education.

Article 12 Every organizer of an education unit is obliged to coordinate with relevant child protection agencies if they find Children dropping out of school due to being victims of acts of violence.

Article 13 Children with disabilities and Children with special needs are given equal opportunities to obtain educational services:
a. formal;
b. non-formal;
c. informal;
d. special education; and e. inclusion.

Article 14 Early Childhood Education aged 0 (zero) to 3 (three) years and 4 (four) to 6 (six) years may be organized by:
a. Posyandu institutions;
b. Posyandu gardens;
c. Early Childhood Education Posts; and d. similar Early Childhood Education unit institutions.

Paragraph 5

Social Welfare Article 15 (1) The implementation of Protection and Empowerment of Children in the field of social welfare as referred to in Article 5 letter d includes:
a. children in conflict with the law;
b. child victims of violence who are economically and sexually exploited;
c. neglected children;
d. child victims of abduction and child victims of human trafficking;
e. child victims of narcotics, alcohol, psychotropic and other addictive substances (NAPZA) abuse;
f. children infected with HIV/AIDS;
g. children who do not have parents and are cared for by other parties or families who are unable;
h. street children;
i. child victims of natural disasters or social disasters; j. children with physical and mental disabilities;
k. children of migrant worker families;
l. children living in or around prostitution locations; and m. child victims of other maltreatment.
2. Social welfare as referred to in paragraph (1) may be in the form of ease of access to services: a. health;
b. education;
c. legal aid;
d. assistance;
e. social, mental and spiritual guidance;
f. social rehabilitation;
g. empowerment;
h. social assistance; and/or i. reintegration.
3. Special infrastructure for persons with disabilities in public places.

Article 16

1. The Regency Government is obligated to protect the fulfillment of the rights of Child workers in the informal sector.
2. Child workers in the informal sector as referred to in paragraph (1) include:a. domestic workers;
b. shoe shiners;
c. street vendors;
d. scavengers;
e. parking attendants;
f. children who work in the fields of agriculture, animal husbandry and plantations;
g. children who work in the field of mining; and h. other informal sector work that employs Children.
3. Every person who employs Children in the informal sector is obligated to observe the requirements:a. minimum age of 15 (fifteen) years, except for the category of Worst Forms of Child Labor minimum 18 (eighteen) years;
b. obtain written consent from the parent/guardian of the Child worker;
c. have a written employment agreement between the employer and the parent/guardian of the Child worker and obtain validation from the authorized agency;
d. not be employed at night;
e. working time of at most 3 (three) hours per day; f. not be employed in a place or environment that can disrupt the child's growth and development process, whether physical, mental, moral and intellectual or the Child's health;
g. provide opportunities to obtain education in accordance with their talents and interests;
h. employ for light types of work; and 1. provide one day off per week.

Article17 Fulfillment of the rights of Child workers in the informal sector as referred to in Article 16 paragraph (1) includes:
a. counseling to stakeholders about the rights of Children;
b. psychological, medical and legal service assistance and socio-economic reintegration;
c. family empowerment through provision of training, business capital stimulus and assistance;
d. scholarships to continue formal education to a higher level; ande. non-formal education and skills training for those who do not pursue formal education.

Part Three Forms of Violence Article18 Forms of violence against Women and Children include: a. physical violence;
b. psychological violence;
c. sexual violence;
d. neglect;
e. exploitation; and /orf. other violence.

Part Four Prevention of Violence against Women and Children Article19 (1) Efforts to prevent violence against Women and Children are carried out in an integrated manner by the Regency Government and coordinated by the technical Regional Apparatus.

2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph (1) are implemented by:a. conducting guidance towards harmonious household integrity;
b. forming a network in efforts to prevent violence;
c. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
d. establishing a violence prevention system;
e. conducting socialization regarding laws and regulations related to the protection of Women and Children victims of violence; andf. providing critical education regarding the rights of Women and Children for the community.

Article20 Efforts to prevent violence against Women and Children as referred to in Article 19 paragraph (1) in addition to the Regency Government are also implemented by:
a. family and/or closest relatives;
b. community;
c. educational institutions; andd. Non-Governmental Organizations.

Part Five

Handling of Women and Children Victims of Violence Article21 Implementation of Handling of Women and Children victims of violence is carried out with:
a. speed;
b. safety and comfort;
c. sense of empathy;
d. non-discrimination;
e. easy accessibility;
f. no charges; andg. guaranteed confidentiality.

Article22 Forms of handling of Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in Article 21 include services:
a. complaints, consultation, and counseling;
b. assistance;
c. medical rehabilitation;
d. social rehabilitation;
e. legal; andf. social reintegration.

CHAPTER III

INTEGRATED SERVICE CENTER (PPT)

Article24 (1) Implementation of services for Women and Children victims of violence is carried out in an integrated manner by the PPT. (2) The PPT as referred to in paragraph (1) is an integrated part and constitutes one unit with the PPT that provides integrated services for witnesses and/or victims of criminal acts of human trafficking. (3) Implementation of services for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. providing fast and free services to victims; b. providing protection and fulfillment of victims' rights to health rehabilitation, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance; c. cooperating with certain institutions in providing translators and volunteer assistants needed for victims;
d. networking with government or private hospitals for treatment and health recovery of victims and cooperating with witness and victim protection institutions, social protection houses or trauma centers owned by the government, community or other institutions for victims' health recovery;
e. providing convenience, comfort and safety for victims;
f. maintaining confidentiality of victims; andg. providing fulfillment of legal assistance for victims. (4) Further provisions regarding procedures and mechanisms for implementing the PPT are regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTERIV

RIGHTS OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE Article25 Every Woman and Child victim of violence has the right: a. to complaint handling;
b. to have their dignity respected as human beings; c. to obtain protection from family, community, regional government and/or other parties either temporarily or based on protection determination from the court; d. to health and psychological recovery from the suffering experienced by the victim;
e. to determine their own decisions;
f. to special handling related to victim confidentiality;
g. to obtain information;
h. to confidentiality of their identity;
1. to social rehabilitation;

J. to assistance by social workers and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of Laws and Regulations; and
k. to religious guidance services.

Article26 Women and children victims of violence in addition to having rights as referred to in Article 25, also receive special rights as follows:
a. right to survival;
b. to grow and develop;
c. to obtain equal protection; andd. to participate in community life.

CHAPTER VI

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 29 (1) The Regent may appoint officials or related Regional Apparatus to conduct guidance and supervision of the implementation of the Protection of Women and Children.

2. Guidance and supervision as referred to in paragraph (1) include: a. provision of implementation instructions;
b. guidance;
c. supervision;
d. monitoring; and e. evaluation of the Implementation of the Protection of Women and Children.

CHAPTER VII

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 30 (1) Community participation in the implementation of Protection of Women and children is as follows:
a. forming family partners at the Village/Sub-district level by the community;
b. conducting independent socialization of women's and children's rights;
c. providing first aid to victims; and d. reporting to the competent authority if violence against victims occurs in their environment. (2) Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by: a. individuals;
b. community institutions;
c. non-governmental organizations;

d. educational institutions;
e. religious institutions;
f. private sector; and/or g. mass media.

CHAPTER VIII

INVESTIGATION PROVISIONS Article 31 (1) Certain Civil Servant Officials within the Regency Government who are granted special authority as Investigators to conduct investigations of criminal acts as regulated in the provisions of laws and regulations.

2. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) are certain Civil Servants within the Regency Government appointed by authorized officials in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations.
3. The authority of Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) includes: a. receiving reports or complaints from a person regarding the existence of a criminal act;
b. taking first action at that time at the scene and conducting an examination;
c. ordering a suspect to stop and examining the suspect's identity card;
d. seizing objects or documents;
e. taking fingerprints and photographing a person;
f. summoning persons to be heard and examined as suspects or witnesses;
g. bringing in experts needed in connection with the examination of the case;
h. conducting termination of investigation after receiving instructions from the Investigator that there is insufficient evidence or the incident does not constitute a criminal act and subsequently through the investigator notifying this to the Public Prosecutor, the suspect or their family;
1. conducting other actions according to law that can be accounted for.
4. Investigators as referred to in paragraph (1) shall notify the commencement of the investigation and submit the results of their investigation to the Public Prosecutor through the Investigator Official of the Police of the Republic of Indonesia, in accordance with the provisions stipulated in the Criminal Procedure Code.
